  "live_records": {
    "q-86680": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86680",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
