    "q-37363": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37363",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $L$ given that \n\n$$\nL[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $L:$\n\n$$\nL={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $L$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $L$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-37363-a",
